1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,233
Feelable. Every diagram, feelable. Snap a textbook diagram, and get a picture a blind student can feel and hear.

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:09,233
Image AI makes amazing pictures: comics, outfit try-ons, room redesigns. But every one of them is made for people who can see.

3
00:00:09,833 --> 00:00:13,233
So we asked the opposite question: who does image AI leave out?

4
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:20,233
Blind students learn from the same textbooks, but every diagram is remade by hand. That takes hours for a complex diagram, around 300 hours per textbook, and students wait weeks.

5
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:26,766
So we use image AI not to make a new picture for the eyes, but to remake a picture for the fingers: a swell-paper print, a 3D relief, and a phone that talks.

6
00:00:27,374 --> 00:00:33,656
Feelable turns a diagram into something a blind student can feel and hear

7
00:00:33,656 --> 00:00:41,891
Ten reviewed lessons: five basic, five complex

8
00:00:42,213 --> 00:00:43,230
A teacher takes a photo of a worksheet Water cycle, photographed on a desk

9
00:00:43,230 --> 00:01:01,461
Real processing, not sped up Read labels → find parts → tactile rules → braille, PDF and 3D

10
00:01:01,461 --> 00:01:07,023
Evaporation, condensation, precipitation and collection become arrows you can follow Every part gets its own texture and a braille label

11
00:01:07,337 --> 00:01:12,129
The hard case: a dense textbook skull with 27 labelled parts

12
00:01:12,129 --> 00:01:13,931
Original on the left, tactile on the right Textures for each bone, sutures as raised lines, braille beside every part

13
00:01:13,931 --> 00:01:16,048
Scan details: what the image AI saw, step by step

14
00:01:16,048 --> 00:01:18,983
Scan details: what the image AI saw, step by step Every label read with neural OCR, blurry words re-read up close

15
00:01:18,983 --> 00:01:21,847
Scan details: what the image AI saw, step by step Every pointer line followed from its label to its tip

16
00:01:21,847 --> 00:01:24,696
Scan details: what the image AI saw, step by step Bones split along their real edges, even when they share a colour

17
00:01:24,696 --> 00:01:28,220
Scan details: what the image AI saw, step by step Sutures traced as the border two bones share

18
00:01:28,536 --> 00:01:30,997
The teacher stays in charge Anything uncertain is listed first, with a fix for each

19
00:01:30,997 --> 00:01:36,626
Draw a missing part on the photo Click inside it and Feelable suggests the outline

20
00:01:36,626 --> 00:01:43,748
Zoom in to check the edge Students only see a lesson after the teacher approves it

21
00:01:44,073 --> 00:01:46,503
Not just shapes: structure A flowchart becomes boxes and arrows a student can follow

22
00:01:46,503 --> 00:01:50,308
“Soil dry? A decision. Yes: open valve. No: log reading.” What the student hears when they touch the diamond

23
00:01:50,602 --> 00:01:52,879
A bus map becomes stations and routes Dashed and solid routes stay distinct by touch, not by colour

24
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:56,486
“Central: an interchange” Feelable works out where routes meet from the picture itself

25
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:01,708
Approve and publish Then print it, 3D-print it, or hand the student a QR code

26
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:20,130
A 3D-printable relief of the same diagram

27
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:41,033
No printer? The student's phone talks. Touch a part to hear its name, double-tap to hear more, and feel a buzz at every edge.

28
00:02:41,633 --> 00:02:48,566
We measure where every outline lands against targets we annotated by hand: valid targets went from 67 to 104 of 142, flowchart arrows 11 of 11, bus routes 15 of 15.

29
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:53,566
Feelable. Every diagram, feelable. Try it at feelable dot onrender dot com.

